### For problems 7–10, use algebraic notation to write a linear function that can be used to model each scenario. Then explain what the slope and the vertical intercept of its graph represent in each problem.

7. At a local pumpkin patch, you can pick your own pumpkins. There is a \$5 charge to enter the patch and a cost of \$0.25 per pound of pumpkin picked. Let $p$ represent the weight in pounds of the pumpkin you choose and $C(p)$ represent the cost in dollars of the pumpkin based on its weight in pounds.

8. You are skiing down a 1,350-meter ski slope at 60 meters per second. Let $t$ represent your skiing time in seconds and $d(t)$ represent the distance in meters from the bottom of the ski slope.

9. The basement of a building is 40 feet below ground level. The building's elevator rises at a rate of 5 feet per second. You enter the elevator in the basement. Let $t$ represent the number of seconds after you press the up button, and let $h(t)$ represent the height of the elevator in feet relative to ground level after $t$ seconds.

10. Several old algebra books are placed in a stack in a library. Each book is $1\frac{1}{4}$ inches thick. Let $b$ represent the number of books in a stack and $s(b)$ represent the total height of the stack in inches.
